          "function_call": null,
          "tool_calls": null,
          "reasoning": "**Contemplating Societal Structures**\n\nI'm currently deeply engaged with the core principles of the Veil of Ignorance. My focus is on evaluating hypothetical societal frameworks, specifically considering how individuals might choose fundamental rights and distributive justice principles when unaware of their own future social standing or personal attributes. The analysis is centered on identifying the most equitable and stable societal configurations that rational actors would endorse.\n\n**Analyzing Social Contracts**\n\nI'm currently dissecting two distinct societal models under the Veil of Ignorance. My analysis is shifting towards a utilitarian framework, weighing the overall well-being and expected utility. I'm prioritizing the evaluation of which society offers a greater aggregate benefit, even if it means a less secure minimum for some, compared to a more equitable but less prosperous alternative.\n\n**Maximizing Expected Utility**\n\nMy thoughts are now firmly on Harsanyi's argument. I'm calculating expected utility, comparing societal models where one offers greater overall prosperity. This involves weighing the potential gains across the majority against the less secure minimum for some, aiming to identify the configuration that rational agents would prefer based on maximizing their average expected well-being.\n\n**Selecting Maximizing Option**\n\nMy focus has now narrowed to a definitive selection between societal models. I am prioritizing the configuration that yields the greatest aggregate well-being and expected value for the average individual. This means favoring increased overall prosperity, even with a less secure minimum for a minority, as the net happiness across the population is maximized.\n\n",
